{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative","id":794344,"title":"Mechanics Vs Narrative","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-12-28T13:05:01-08:00","id":794344,"title":"Mechanics Vs Narrative","content":"Does anyone else feel slightly urked about how cWOD will sometimes through narrative under the bus for it's mechanics and vise versa?<br /> <br /> Like when in vampire all &quot;Plot&quot; NPC's have similar numbers of backgrounds and disciplines regardless of whether they are a powerful elder more ancient than the roman empire or a neonate that was embraced 4 years ago(mechanics screwing narrative). Or when they don't give stats to NPC's because they're &quot;Too powerful&quot; to be measured(Narrative screwing mechanics).<br /> <br /> It all rings of an attempt to &quot;Balance&quot; things that don't really need it, in doing so limiting both the storyteller and players in what they can achieve. While I know I can house rule this stuff out, it still bugs me when the &quot;Official&quot; rules do this. <br /> <br /> What are your guy's thoughts? How do you handle your stories when a splat has mechanics that don't allow you to do what they story says they should or vise versa?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-28T23:19:02-08:00","id":794640,"title":"","content":"I revise or ignore the stats. The rules exist to facilitate the story, not vice versa.<br /> <br /> But the story should never dis-empower players. I don't have any objections to saying an NPC is too powerful for stats, but I'm not going to use them in direct contests against PCs, run scenes where the PCs aren't supposed to do anything but gawk at how great and powerful the NPC is, etc. Some of the CWoD's adventures were pretty bad on that front (lookin' at you, Transylvania Chronicles). If I do use a Methuselah or uber-powerful elder directly on-stage, it'll be under circumstances where the PCs can still make meaningful choices that aren't limited to &quot;do what the uber-NPC wants or get Dominated/die horribly.&quot; It's not impossible to do, either. Lair of the Hidden was an awesomely-written adventure that prominently featured two entities too powerful for game stats, yet put the outcome of events squarely in the PCs' hands.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://blood-and-bourbon.obsidianportal.com/\" target=\"_blank\">Blood and Bourbon</a>, my New Orleans-based Vampire chronicle.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3110-false-epiphany","name":"False Epiphany","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3110_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"748"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-28T23:36:38-08:00","id":794643,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative#post794344\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">What are your guy's thoughts? How do you handle your stories when a splat has mechanics that don't allow you to do what they story says they should or vise versa?</div> </div> </div> </div>This has never happened to me. As a storyteller, I am in control of what my supernatural beasties are capable of. I have used sorcerers to play the role of 'mages' so they would not run amok, and I have made up my own spells, rituals, spirit charms and gifts as necessary.<br /> <br /> Statless (or practically statless) monsters are fine, but they are best used as half-glimpsed leviathans swimming through the murky background. If brought into the spotlight they are less mysterious and enthralling. I don't care how scary the Seven-fold Curse is, if you pass Caine every Friday on the way to the bloodbank he gets a lot less frightening. <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3695-the-laughing-stranger","name":"The Laughing Stranger","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar3695_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1010"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-29T00:23:55-08:00","id":794657,"title":"","content":"When you're <i>writing</i> a story, the narrative trumps everything. There might be setting rules, a &quot;bible&quot; for the series, but those rules can be changed if necessary. Look at all the ridiculous plot holes in TV series and movies that exist because, if people acted reasonably, or the McGuffin from last week's episode still existed, the story couldn't be told. Or look at comics and how in one issue, Captain America can lift a car, but in the next he's trapped under a 300 pound wooden beam. The story needs to get told, so the story gets told.<br /> <br /> Roleplaying games, though, are not <i>just</i> about <i>writing</i> a story. They're also a game, played by multiple people, working together to have fun. The Storyteller is there to challenge the players and present them with an interesting setting to engage with. So you run into a conflict. If story is all that matters, then rules <i>don't matter</i>... except they do, because that's how the <i>players</i> interact with the setting. They rely on knowing that when they roll their dice, a success means success and a failure means failure. They rely on knowing that if they can lift 500 pounds today, they can lift 500 pounds tomorrow. The Storyteller can't say &quot;Well, yes, I know you could bench press a car last week, but this week I need you to be trapped under a wooden beam. So you can't accomplish that feat of strength right now, or my story is ruined.&quot;<br /> <br /> The rules provide a framework that the story <i><b>must</b></i> exist <b><i>inside</i></b>. A well-written game does that. It provides a consistent baseline framework that everyone, NPC and PC, adheres to. And, hopefully, one that is <i>fair</i>. Not fair to the characters, but fair to the <i>players</i>. That means that if your character is a 20-year old marine with badass combat capabilities and equipment, you can expect to face down an NPC who is a 20-year old marine with badass combat capabilities and equipment and have it be a fair fight. It means that if the Storyteller wants to have one single NPC take on an entire party of PCs, the NPC needs an advantage that makes sense inside the framework. They need more resources, more experience, more powers, more goons. <br /> <br /> A lazy way of handling this is to make NPC-only-powers that handle this issue for you. Want an elite NPC to thrash your entire party of PC's? Give that NPC abilities that the PC's can't match. Ones that are intentionally, absolutely <i>stronger</i> than the equivalent abilities that PC's have access to. Unfortunately, White Wolf tends to go this way. A <i>lot</i>. Vampire is <i>full</i> of Disciplines that are just flat <i>better</i> than others, because they were originally designed for use by NPCs only. They were meant to trash entire groups of PC's. Quietus, Vicissitude, Chimerstry, Serpentis and Obtenebration are all great examples. They work fine, <i>if they are only ever used by NPCs to advance the plot. </i>They give the Storyteller immense freedom and latitude in what can be accomplished with them (especially Chimerstry and Vicissitude) because they are specifically designed as plot advancement tools. <br /> <br /> It requires more creativity to have only <i>one</i> set of tools for both NPCs and PCs, because it means that everyone is on the same playing field. Then you're stuck with boring things like giving the NPC's lots of experience, or more resources, or more goons, as noted above. Which doesn't always make sense. Sometimes it'd be a great story if there was just this <i>one</i> bad guy who, all alone, presented a challenge to an entire group of PCs, but was still kind of on their &quot;level&quot;. About their age, about their standing in society, from a similar background. Except he needs <i>more power</i> than the PC's have, but there's no logical way to explain <i>why</i> he should have that power. He just <i>needs</i> it for the plot to work. <br /> <br /> To me, that kind of story is a bad story. The rules <i>should</i> be fair. NPCs and PCs should use the same toolkit, and that means that if the Storyteller wants to challenge the PCs, the Storyteller has to use the same tools. She might give her NPC's more money, or more contacts, or they might be older, more experienced, better trained.. but they shouldn't simply be <i>stronger</i> without logic behind it.<br /> <br /> If you have to change the rules just so your plot works, maybe you should consider changing your plot instead? Because, hey, what if your players outsmart you? What if they have a perfectly good reason to learn whatever magic your NPC is using? What if they obtain the magical device powering her army? What if they convert the NPC's overpowered minion to their side? <br /> <br /> Basically, the setting might be unfair, but the rules should not be. And the rules should stay the rules. They're your limits. Your plot can bend them a bit, but they exist for a reason. They make sure that what happens this time on As The Dice Roll is consistent with what happened last time. They're the glue that keeps the bottom from falling out of your plot. <br /> <br /> If you want a necromancer to threaten your party, that necromancer should be built using the same rules as your players used to build their own characters. If he's stronger than they are, why? Is he older? Is he better trained? Does he have a mentor backing his play? Because if you can't answer that question, you have a problem. And I have a problem with you, too. Isn't it fair of the players to ask &quot;Why is he better than me?&quot; and expect an answer back? If the bad guy can master Necromancy by age 22, why can't my character? That is not a silly question.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-29T08:36:35-08:00","id":794851,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=794657#post794657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Roleplaying games, though, are not <i>just</i> about <i>writing</i> a story. They're also a game, played by multiple people, working together to have fun. The Storyteller is there to challenge the players and present them with an interesting setting to engage with. So you run into a conflict. If story is all that matters, then rules <i>don't matter</i>... except they do, because that's how the <i>players</i> interact with the setting. They rely on knowing that when they roll their dice, a success means success and a failure means failure. They rely on knowing that if they can lift 500 pounds today, they can lift 500 pounds tomorrow. The Storyteller can't say &quot;Well, yes, I know you could bench press a car last week, but this week I need you to be trapped under a wooden beam. So you can't accomplish that feat of strength right now, or my story is ruined.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>Actually, this sort of can happen. Not because of story fluff, but because the player failed a strength role on something that he should have logically been able to do. Just one of the qwerks about basing game mechanics around dice rolls<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=794657#post794657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A lazy way of handling this is to make NPC-only-powers that handle this issue for you. Want an elite NPC to thrash your entire party of PC's? Give that NPC abilities that the PC's can't match. Ones that are intentionally, absolutely <i>stronger</i> than the equivalent abilities that PC's have access to. Unfortunately, White Wolf tends to go this way. A <i>lot</i>. Vampire is <i>full</i> of Disciplines that are just flat <i>better</i> than others, because they were originally designed for use by NPCs only. They were meant to trash entire groups of PC's. Quietus, Vicissitude, Chimerstry, Serpentis and Obtenebration are all great examples. They work fine, <i>if they are only ever used by NPCs to advance the plot. </i>They give the Storyteller immense freedom and latitude in what can be accomplished with them (especially Chimerstry and Vicissitude) because they are specifically designed as plot advancement tools.</div> </div> </div> </div>Though pc's can play sabbat and/or learn these disciplines if they find the right teacher.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=794657#post794657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It requires more creativity to have only <i>one</i> set of tools for both NPCs and PCs, because it means that everyone is on the same playing field. Then you're stuck with boring things like giving the NPC's lots of experience, or more resources, or more goons, as noted above. Which doesn't always make sense. Sometimes it'd be a great story if there was just this <i>one</i> bad guy who, all alone, presented a challenge to an entire group of PCs, but was still kind of on their &quot;level&quot;. About their age, about their standing in society, from a similar background. Except he needs <i>more power</i> than the PC's have, but there's no logical way to explain <i>why</i> he should have that power. He just <i>needs</i> it for the plot to work.</div> </div> </div> </div>It can be explained logically if he had these connections Before he was embraced, but no matter how much of a big shot you were in life you still shouldn't compare to an ancient monster who's been secretly running society from the shadows since carthage.<br /> <br /> Though this is why I found the experience systems in most games(not just WOD) to be complete bullsh**. They always boiled down to either &quot;buy X with Y number of experience points and suddenly you have Z advantage with little to no in story context of how you got it&quot; or &quot;While yes you do have many in story friends to help you/reason you should know this power/background experience necessary to recognize X, you haven't paid the necessary experience points yet so you can't use it.&quot; It all seemed so incredibly arbitrary to me.<br /> <br /> I tend to just throw out such systems completely and substitute by having players make &quot;(relevent attribute) + empathy&quot; rolls if they have a teacher(Base difficulty determined by mentor level) or if without a teacher &quot;(relevant trait)&quot; role at base difficulty 10(can be lowered by other factors). The player must accumulate a number of successes equal to the experience cost of desired power, Player may not spend willpower, can only role 1-2 times an in game day(each role represents hours of study), and botches indicate a loss from your success pool equal to the number of 1's rolled.<br /> <br /> Backgrounds on the other hand must be accumulated logically in-story. Though I do give players the knowledges academics 2(everyone goes to school), drive 2(unless they have the child flaw), computers 2, and occult 1(everyone knows about the modern vampire/werewolf/ghost myths) for free. I also give adults resources 2(a stable job). It always bothered me that the game didn't account for the fact that your character should have Existed before their embrace/awakening/first transformation in the character creation rules.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-29T16:42:20-08:00","id":795190,"title":"","content":"Originally, in VtM 1st Edition, the Sabbat Clans, Independent Clans and all the Bloodlines were <i><b>not</b></i> playable options. They were for NPC use only. That continued to be pretty strongly encouraged until well into the later half of 2nd edition. Even Revised had a really strong vibe of &quot;these are not for you&quot; when talking about anything but the Camarilla Clans.<br /> <br /> The idea behind Experience is <i>time</i>. Sure, you might know people, but if you haven't spent the XP on acquiring them as a Contact or Ally or whatever, then you just haven't gotten to the point where they're comfortable giving you that help. You just not to each other as you pass on the street. Or they can't make time for you. Maybe in the game you <i>talked</i> about taking time out to learn kung fu, but if you didn't spend the XP, maybe you never really learned your lessons, or you procrastinated and flunked your Kung Fu Exam. Experience buys traits that your character has actually <i>earned</i>. Even if it might make sense for them to have a trait, if they never burned XP on it, they obviously didn't bother to invest time in it.<br /> <br /> I've been around IT professionals for years, but haven't picked up any IT-related skills because I haven't invested the time in listening to them or helping them out. Sure, it would make <i>sense</i> for me to be <i>able</i> to draw on those contacts and improve my own IT abilities... but I'm not interested, so I haven't done that. I've been doing other things.<br /> <br /> XP makes sense, if you bother to work it into the story. <br /> <br /> Giving away free points seems silly. Some people didn't finish high school, some people didn't learn to drive, my mother barely knows what a werewolf is. I know a fair number of people that don't have Resources 2, even as adults. There are people that don't have Resources 1. There are homeless people and people living in extreme poverty. If you spend all your traits on something else, then your character just never did those things and earned those dots. It's resource management. The character creation system <i>does</i> account for your character's mortal life. It just lets the <i>player choose what that life was</i>. There are plenty of concepts where Academics 0, Drive 0, Computer 0, Occult 0 and Resources 0 make sense.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I'm a professor! Why is no one listening to me?!","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/235-telgar","name":"Telgar","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar235_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2506"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T04:33:28-08:00","id":796775,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=795190#post795190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Originally, in VtM 1st Edition, the Sabbat Clans, Independent Clans and all the Bloodlines were <i><b>not</b></i> playable options. They were for NPC use only. That continued to be pretty strongly encouraged until well into the later half of 2nd edition. Even Revised had a really strong vibe of &amp;quot;these are not for you&amp;quot; when talking about anything but the Camarilla Clans.<br /> <br /> The idea behind Experience is <i>time</i>. Sure, you might know people, but if you haven't spent the XP on acquiring them as a Contact or Ally or whatever, then you just haven't gotten to the point where they're comfortable giving you that help. You just not to each other as you pass on the street. Or they can't make time for you. Maybe in the game you <i>talked</i> about taking time out to learn kung fu, but if you didn't spend the XP, maybe you never really learned your lessons, or you procrastinated and flunked your Kung Fu Exam. Experience buys traits that your character has actually <i>earned</i>. Even if it might make sense for them to have a trait, if they never burned XP on it, they obviously didn't bother to invest time in it.<br /> <br /> I've been around IT professionals for years, but haven't picked up any IT-related skills because I haven't invested the time in listening to them or helping them out. Sure, it would make <i>sense</i> for me to be <i>able</i> to draw on those contacts and improve my own IT abilities... but I'm not interested, so I haven't done that. I've been doing other things.<br /> <br /> XP makes sense, if you bother to work it into the story. <br /> <br /> Giving away free points seems silly. Some people didn't finish high school, some people didn't learn to drive, my mother barely knows what a werewolf is. I know a fair number of people that don't have Resources 2, even as adults. There are people that don't have Resources 1. There are homeless people and people living in extreme poverty. If you spend all your traits on something else, then your character just never did those things and earned those dots. It's resource management. The character creation system <i>does</i> account for your character's mortal life. It just lets the <i>player choose what that life was</i>. There are plenty of concepts where Academics 0, Drive 0, Computer 0, Occult 0 and Resources 0 make sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>Also, vampires (and other critters) might have been made before cars existed, and probably discarded a lot of their mortal lives upon the Embrace. So Drive, Computers, Academics and Resources might not be appropriate.<br /> <br /> There is a tendency for these traits to be overlooked anyway, but it largely depends on how strict the ST is. If you don't take any Resources, you shouldn't complain if the ST doesn't let you buy anything.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Writer, publisher, performer<br /> Mostly he/his, sometimes she/her IRL <a href=\"https://adam-lowe.com\" target=\"_blank\">https://adam-lowe.com</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5291-adambeyoncelowe","name":"adambeyoncelowe","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5291_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2802"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T07:07:58-08:00","id":796828,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=795190#post795190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The idea behind Experience is <i>time</i>. Sure, you might know people, but if you haven't spent the XP on acquiring them as a Contact or Ally or whatever, then you just haven't gotten to the point where they're comfortable giving you that help. You just not to each other as you pass on the street. Or they can't make time for you. Maybe in the game you <i>talked</i> about taking time out to learn kung fu, but if you didn't spend the XP, maybe you never really learned your lessons, or you procrastinated and flunked your Kung Fu Exam. Experience buys traits that your character has actually <i>earned</i>. Even if it might make sense for them to have a trait, if they never burned XP on it, they obviously didn't bother to invest time in it.</div> </div> </div> </div><span style=\"color:#252C2F\"><span style=\"font-family:Helvetica\"><span style=\"font-size:13px\">This is why I created the roll system to represent time and investment. There are plenty of WOD NPC's who have far more dots in disciplines/gifts/etc. than the experience system would allow. As you said before, evening the playing field between NPCs and PCs of similar age and resources.</span></span></span><br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=795190#post795190\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Giving away free points seems silly. Some people didn't finish high school, some people didn't learn to drive, my mother barely knows what a werewolf is. I know a fair number of people that don't have Resources 2, even as adults. There are people that don't have Resources 1. There are homeless people and people living in extreme poverty. If you spend all your traits on something else, then your character just never did those things and earned those dots. It's resource management. The character creation system <i>does</i> account for your character's mortal life. It just lets the <i>player choose what that life was</i>. There are plenty of concepts where Academics 0, Drive 0, Computer 0, Occult 0 and Resources 0 make sense.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yes there are some people who failed high school, never learned to drive, never owned a computer/smartphone/etc., or never managed to gain a job as an adult. This is however a Minority of people. Though there are some clans that would favor embracing such outcasts, many clans(ventrue lambrosa, tsimcze, etc.) and potential sires would never consider embracing someone unless they showed At Least this level of competence in mortal society.<br /> <br /> I won't do it if it doesn't make sense with their character concept.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T07:15:17-08:00","id":796831,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>adambeyoncelowe</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=796775#post796775\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, vampires (and other critters) might have been made before cars existed, and probably discarded a lot of their mortal lives upon the Embrace. So Drive, Computers, Academics and Resources might not be appropriate.<br /> <br /> There is a tendency for these traits to be overlooked anyway, but it largely depends on how strict the ST is. If you don't take any Resources, you shouldn't complain if the ST doesn't let you buy anything.</div> </div> </div> </div>Considering how long cars have been around(since their invention in 1885) and how deeply tied they are to mortal society, unless the player is more than a century old and wealthy enough to afford a chauffeur/lives in an area with reliable buses(pffft) or a subway, they'd Have to learn.(Unless you're running a dark ages game)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T07:56:45-08:00","id":796838,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Telgar</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=794657#post794657\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">When you're <i>writing</i> a story, the narrative trumps everything. There might be setting rules, a &quot;bible&quot; for the series, but those rules can be changed if necessary. Look at all the ridiculous plot holes in TV series and movies that exist because, if people acted reasonably, or the McGuffin from last week's episode still existed, the story couldn't be told. Or look at comics and how in one issue, Captain America can lift a car, but in the next he's trapped under a 300 pound wooden beam. The story needs to get told, so the story gets told.<br /> <br /> Roleplaying games, though, are not <i>just</i> about <i>writing</i> a story. They're also a game, played by multiple people, working together to have fun. The Storyteller is there to challenge the players and present them with an interesting setting to engage with. So you run into a conflict. If story is all that matters, then rules <i>don't matter</i>... except they do, because that's how the <i>players</i> interact with the setting. They rely on knowing that when they roll their dice, a success means success and a failure means failure. They rely on knowing that if they can lift 500 pounds today, they can lift 500 pounds tomorrow. The Storyteller can't say &quot;Well, yes, I know you could bench press a car last week, but this week I need you to be trapped under a wooden beam. So you can't accomplish that feat of strength right now, or my story is ruined.&quot;<br /> <br /> The rules provide a framework that the story <i><b>must</b></i> exist <b><i>inside</i></b>. A well-written game does that. It provides a consistent baseline framework that everyone, NPC and PC, adheres to. And, hopefully, one that is <i>fair</i>. Not fair to the characters, but fair to the <i>players</i>. That means that if your character is a 20-year old marine with badass combat capabilities and equipment, you can expect to face down an NPC who is a 20-year old marine with badass combat capabilities and equipment and have it be a fair fight. It means that if the Storyteller wants to have one single NPC take on an entire party of PCs, the NPC needs an advantage that makes sense inside the framework. They need more resources, more experience, more powers, more goons.<br /> <br /> A lazy way of handling this is to make NPC-only-powers that handle this issue for you. Want an elite NPC to thrash your entire party of PC's? Give that NPC abilities that the PC's can't match. Ones that are intentionally, absolutely <i>stronger</i> than the equivalent abilities that PC's have access to. Unfortunately, White Wolf tends to go this way. A <i>lot</i>. Vampire is <i>full</i> of Disciplines that are just flat <i>better</i> than others, because they were originally designed for use by NPCs only. They were meant to trash entire groups of PC's. Quietus, Vicissitude, Chimerstry, Serpentis and Obtenebration are all great examples. They work fine, <i>if they are only ever used by NPCs to advance the plot. </i>They give the Storyteller immense freedom and latitude in what can be accomplished with them (especially Chimerstry and Vicissitude) because they are specifically designed as plot advancement tools.<br /> <br /> It requires more creativity to have only <i>one</i> set of tools for both NPCs and PCs, because it means that everyone is on the same playing field. Then you're stuck with boring things like giving the NPC's lots of experience, or more resources, or more goons, as noted above. Which doesn't always make sense. Sometimes it'd be a great story if there was just this <i>one</i> bad guy who, all alone, presented a challenge to an entire group of PCs, but was still kind of on their &quot;level&quot;. About their age, about their standing in society, from a similar background. Except he needs <i>more power</i> than the PC's have, but there's no logical way to explain <i>why</i> he should have that power. He just <i>needs</i> it for the plot to work.<br /> <br /> To me, that kind of story is a bad story. The rules <i>should</i> be fair. NPCs and PCs should use the same toolkit, and that means that if the Storyteller wants to challenge the PCs, the Storyteller has to use the same tools. She might give her NPC's more money, or more contacts, or they might be older, more experienced, better trained.. but they shouldn't simply be <i>stronger</i> without logic behind it.<br /> <br /> If you have to change the rules just so your plot works, maybe you should consider changing your plot instead? Because, hey, what if your players outsmart you? What if they have a perfectly good reason to learn whatever magic your NPC is using? What if they obtain the magical device powering her army? What if they convert the NPC's overpowered minion to their side?<br /> <br /> Basically, the setting might be unfair, but the rules should not be. And the rules should stay the rules. They're your limits. Your plot can bend them a bit, but they exist for a reason. They make sure that what happens this time on As The Dice Roll is consistent with what happened last time. They're the glue that keeps the bottom from falling out of your plot.<br /> <br /> If you want a necromancer to threaten your party, that necromancer should be built using the same rules as your players used to build their own characters. If he's stronger than they are, why? Is he older? Is he better trained? Does he have a mentor backing his play? Because if you can't answer that question, you have a problem. And I have a problem with you, too. Isn't it fair of the players to ask &quot;Why is he better than me?&quot; and expect an answer back? If the bad guy can master Necromancy by age 22, why can't my character? That is not a silly question.</div> </div> </div> </div>I had a somewhat similar example, i played a campaign with a DM that was.....well as gronard as you can get, his favority justification for everything was &quot;i have being DM since before you were born and thus i am right&quot; yeah, that kind of DM.<br /> <br /> So he use a &quot;realistic&quot; approach for expending XP. His logic went &quot;3 in a skill is professional level, you need 10+ years to become a true professional in something. The game was played in real time so noone could really raise to 3 if they didnt do it from chargen and forget about 4. Which wasnt really bad by itself, if we all played under the same rules.<br /> <br /> But then, he had NPC with 5 in skills, because said NPCs were hermits who trained since infancy in the use of the sword. As a side note we had to make &quot;real people&quot; first and then splats. Then the net result was the PC were always inferior to the shinning NPCs he put in there because said NPCs were around 10 years before the game. His requirement for justification for skill and attributes were highly restrictive for PC but not for his NPCs because &quot;they story need it&quot;<br /> <br /> With attributes he argue that he couldnt think &quot;how you can raise intelligence, as people cannot train to raise their IQ&quot; he used the 2nd edition definition of the attributes so metal and social attributes couldnt be raise during play.<br /> <br /> Except his NPCs, oh no, in a party of a collegue kid, a police rookie, a street girl and a metalhead the NPC who was a kung fu skater prodigy biker with 5 in dex and 4 in melee which also was socially undervelop but also was full of sazz. He stick up like a sore thumb.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 12-30-2015, 12:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T12:56:31-08:00","id":796979,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=796838#post796838\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I had a somewhat similar example, i played a campaign with a DM that was.....well as gronard as you can get, his favority justification for everything was &quot;i have being DM since before you were born and thus i am right&quot; yeah, that kind of DM.<br /> <br /> So he use a &quot;realistic&quot; approach for expending XP. His logic went &quot;3 in a skill is professional level, you need 10+ years to become a true professional in something. The game was played in real time so noone could really raise to 3 if they didnt do it from chargen and forget about 4. Which wasnt really bad by itself, if we all played under the same rules.<br /> <br /> But then, he had NPC with 5 in skills, because said NPCs were hermits who trained since infancy in the use of the sword. As a side note we had to make &quot;real people&quot; first and then splats. Then the net result was the PC were always inferior to the shinning NPCs he put in there because said NPCs were around 10 years before the game. His requirement for justification for skill and attributes were highly restrictive for PC but not for his NPCs because &quot;they story need it&quot;<br /> <br /> With attributes he argue that he couldnt think &quot;how you can raise intelligence, as people cannot train to raise their IQ&quot; he used the 2nd edition definition of the attributes so metal and social attributes couldnt be raise during play.<br /> <br /> Except his NPCs, oh no, in a party of a collegue kid, a police rookie, a street girl and a metalhead the NPC who was a kung fu skater prodigy biker with 5 in dex and 4 in melee which also was socially undervelop but also was full of sazz. He stick up like a sore thumb.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> 3 as professional? Considering 5 is mastery and being a professional means you've mastered your craft.... I'd personally disagree.<br /> <br /> On you other points it sounded like he was just being an ass-hat. A random skater shouldn't have those kinds of stats because he's a &quot;Prodigy&quot; when the players get screwed like that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T15:17:07-08:00","id":797037,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=796979#post796979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3 as professional? Considering 5 is mastery and being a professional means you've mastered your craft.... I'd personally disagree.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe im using the term wrong here but professional doesnt mean &quot;you can make a living doing this&quot;? I mean i do make a living as an accountant but i havent mastered accounting, i dont write book about accounting, nor i am an authority of the &quot;accounting world&quot;. <br /> <br /> 3 is &quot;i make a living of this&quot; professional<br /> 4 is &quot;i wrote book about it&quot; professional<br /> 5 is &quot;Im the best in the world&quot; professional.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=796979#post796979\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">On you other points it sounded like he was just being an ass-hat. A random skater shouldn't have those kinds of stats because he's a &quot;Prodigy&quot; when the players get screwed like that.</div> </div> </div> </div>Oh wish he was a random skater. No, he was a guy trained from birth into being a kung fu expert &amp; master skater. All to the ripe age of 14. With dex 5 and melee 4. Again, not bad if the tone of the game was a throw-realism-out-of-the-window kinda game. But it was &quot;realistic&quot; game.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":2179,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","user":"LokiRavenSpeak","date":". 12-30-2015, 06:21 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T16:51:03-08:00","id":797079,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=797037#post797037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Oh wish he was a random skater. No, he was a guy trained from birth into being a kung fu expert &amp; master skater. All to the ripe age of 14. With dex 5 and melee 4. Again, not bad if the tone of the game was a throw-realism-out-of-the-window kinda game. But it was &quot;realistic&quot; game.</div> </div> </div> </div>Master Skater? I'm sorry, but that couldn't have been any more imaginary if you replaced it with &quot;Purple Unicorn&quot;. What was your DM thinking?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6383-prometheas","name":"Prometheas","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1101"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T17:20:52-08:00","id":797086,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Prometheas</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=797079#post797079\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Master Skater? I'm sorry, but that couldn't have been any more imaginary if you replaced it with &quot;Purple Unicorn&quot;. What was your DM thinking?</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly i believe his thinking is that the story is not about the PC or the players, we just along for the ride to see THE SUPAH AWRSOME!!!!!!! NPCS plots. You want a magic sword? Sorry fluff says they supposse to be super rare to find.....except for the NPCs they had 4 because its &quot;justified&quot; in their background because thats....&quot;fun&quot; in a very very &quot;you wouldnt believe this shit&quot; kinda fun.<br /> <br /> They get to be special and we get to be the mundame guys who awe at the awesomeness of them. Granted he run some impressive plot and twist......but they always rely on NPCs starting, moving and finishing said plots. With our PCs only ever allowed to do plot if A) We run plots that are deeply personal and dont affect the &quot;main&quot; plot or B) We find the correct pixel.<br /> <br /> But thats derailing a little, point was that as telgar says NPC and PCs should move on the same rules otherwise it feels unfair or if they gotta play with different rules it should favor the players.<br /> <br /> That said, the ST system handles progression pretty badly. Specially with inmortals like vampires.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2179-lokiravenspeak","name":"LokiRavenSpeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1117"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-30T22:14:07-08:00","id":797194,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>LokiRavenSpeak</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/794344-mechanics-vs-narrative?p=797037#post797037\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">3 is &quot;i make a living of this&quot; professional<br /> 4 is &quot;i wrote book about it&quot; professional<br /> 5 is &quot;Im the best in the world&quot; professional.</div> </div> </div> </div>This is more or less how I explain things to newer players. From there though, natural talent (attributes) and possible merits/flaws might complicate matters a bit. But this description (or close to it) has worked for simplified explanations.<br /> <br /> Oh...also seconding Telgar's entire first post. Everyone should play by the same rules. Come up with story reasons why an NPC of similar age/experience is better than they should be.<br /> <br /> Also, be prepared if it's an in story exploitable reason for players to attempt to do the same. My first Vampire ST got very frustrated when he came up with a BS reason an NPC was far more powerful than they had rights to be, and after the story some characters went and pursued the same paths. This lead to the game being thrown way out of balance from what had been planned.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://drive.google.com/uc?export=view&amp;id=0B9RzepSqCby3Mi1KUmJ5aWUwRVk\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":805,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","user":"idpersona","date":". 12-31-2015, 01:20 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/805-idpersona","name":"idpersona","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar805_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2117"}]},"thank